abstract: >-
  A reusable toolkit (redaction_audit) that tests whether redacted action logs still let
  a reviewer verify an authorisation policy, using evidence needs, redaction declarations
  and generated boundary violations, and reports what the logs expose. It ships with
  a reproducible pilot (Python 3 standard library only, synthetic data) that
  measures whether a reviewer can verify consent for an AI agent's completed
  document share after the audit log is redacted. Twenty scripted runs (ten
  matched authorised/unauthorised pairs) are rendered as full logs, uniformly
  masked logs and logs with consistent within-run aliases, plus two ablations
  that omit expiry or revocation evidence. Reported metrics: identical-input
  pairs, a forced-binary accuracy ceiling, three-valued rule-reviewer outcomes
  and direct exposure of planted values.
